Z||000012||sluppy123||03-23-2001||05:17 PM||||I'm with 1FST2M6, 87 GT's all the way.<P><BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=247832&a=7653930&p=30140375&Sequence=0&res"> <BR><P>------------------<BR>-Brian, Toolmaker<BR>Lincoln, NE<BR>85' SC & 87' GT-Leather, Lowered, Nitrous<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=247832&a=7653930&p=37756786&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||TwisT||03-23-2001||07:33 PM||||OK.  I own an '88 GT and I love the hell out of it.  Handles and rides like a dream,  43,000 miles on her.  I love it.  I love it I love it I love it...<P>BUT...  IMHO,  the best Fiero to buy would be the '86/'87 GT.  Why?  Cuz they're cheap and much easier to find aftermarket parts for.  Plus,  I love the fastback design.  These cars are all over the place.  Yes the '88's have great suspension,  as well as various other improvements,  but they tend to be pricey and harder to find parts for.  Find a great deal on one (like I did) then buy it.  Otherwise...<P>If I did it all over again,  I'd have me an '87 GT with a 4.10 4-speed tranny installed.  Guys,  if you HAVEN'T experienced the difference between the 4-speed and 5-speed,  YOU ARE MISSING OUT!  With the 4-speed Fiero,  it feels like and pulls like you are driving a genuine muscle car.  The 5-speed tends to be pretty tame when compared.  It's all about gearing...<P>Brett<P>||||reg||1
